ed species persistence under a metapopulation or species-distribution model with connectivity considerations).\n\nHousehold location choice is modeled by a social/economic model: there is a population of households $H$, and each household $h\in H$ choosing to locate at parcel $i$ at time $t$ derives utility $U_{hit}(\mathbf{y},\mathbf{x},\mathbf{p})$, where $\mathbf{p}$ are endogenous land prices/rents determined by a market-clearing or equilibrium condition (e.g., a discrete-choice/logit residential sorting model, a bid-rent equilibrium, or a dynamic Tiebout-type model). Aggregate household welfare is $W(\mathbf{x},\mathbf{y},\mathbf{p}) = \sum_{h,i,t} \pi_{hit} U_{hit}$, where $\pi_{hit}$ are equilibrium choice probabilities.\n\nThe development pattern $\mathbf{y}$ is jointly determined with $\mathbf{p}$ by the residential choice equilibrium, and is influenced by the reserve decisions $\mathbf{x}$ (which remove parcels from the developable pool and alter location amenities and prices).\n\nProblem. Formulate and solve a planner's dynamic reserve-selection problem of the form\n$$\\max_{\\mathbf{x}} \\; \\alpha\\, B(\\mathbf{x}) + (1-\\alpha)\\, W(\\mathbf{x},\\mathbf{y}^*(\\mathbf{x}),\\mathbf{p}^*(\\mathbf{x}))$$\nsubject to budget, area, and connectivity constraints on $\\mathbf{x}$, where $(\\mathbf{y}^*(\\mathbf{x}),\\mathbf{p}^*(\\mathbf{x}))$ is the residential-choice equilibrium induced by $\\mathbf{x}$, and $\\alpha\\in[0,1]$ trades off biodiversity against household welfare. Provide an integrated mathematical framework combining social/economic residential-location-choice models with dynamic reserve-selection optimization, together with algorithms that compute optimal or provably good reserve policies under the induced bilevel/equilibrium structure.","status_search_result":"The question is posed as an open research challenge in operations research for forestry/conservation planning. While there is a substantial literature on reserve site selection and on residential sorting models separately, a fully integrated dynamic framework jointly optimizing household welfare and biodiversity through reserve selection with endogenous residential choice does not appear to have an accepted general solution. Status remains open.","status_evidence":[{"title":"Operations Research challenges in forestry: 33 open problems","url":"https://zbmath.org/6501877","snippet":"List of 33 open problems including incorporation of residential location choice into dynamic reserve selection.","claim":"The problem is listed as an open research challenge."}]},"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"c44af7d8-3ada-4755-a3bb-d9464ea80ff2"}
